# function gather(r) { .. Promise.all([r]); .. }   over records { n: i + work };   and the test says: Promise.resolve = function (value) { value.n = "t"; return original.call(this, value); }
# Promise.all is native code that looks up this.resolve and hands it each element. An executable locks that member; a test run lets a test replace it.
# The rows of the combinator say that the elements are only stored, which holds of the member as built alone: with it replaced the records leave, and nothing is proven of their field.
# Before, the field was proven a number, and the store of a text in the stand-in stopped the run: "StoreOfOtherType of an object of layout L0 ..: the compiler has proven that it never happens".
# The wire lists Promise.resolve alone as replaced: a front end that lists Promise.all beside it hides the question.
# OPTIONS: admit=ReplacedBuiltins
# RUN: link fields layouts mechanisms=all
# CHECK: field L0.0 "n" of-birth
# CHECK-NOT: layout L0 changes=[] stays-with-compiled-code

